────────────────────────────────────────── Final Decision Document ───────────────────────────────────────────
# Deliberation Decision Document
**Feature Request:** The CRM should alert us when our relationship with a country goes cold.

**Termination Reason:** `CONVERGENCE_ALL_ISSUES_RESOLVED_OR_ESCALATED (CRITIC_SIGNALED_CONVERGENCE)` | **Final
Disagreement Score:** `0.0` | **Confidence (Proposer / Critic):** `0.72` / `0.35`

## Executive Summary
v1 will add a weekly cold-country alert built entirely from existing CRM data. A country is flagged cold when 
its last qualifying Engagement, or its onboarding date if later, is more than 90 days old. The flag is 
suppressed only while the country has an Active Mission updated within 180 days. Coordinators receive an email
with only a count and a link, and the country detail appears in-app behind login. Countries with no assigned 
or active coordinator go to an Operations fallback section. The main trade-off argued was alert accuracy and 
confidentiality against adding no new data entry or workflow. All five challenges (C1-C5) ended accepted, so 
no scope or success criteria are pending and no open questions remain for humans.

## 1. Agreed Scope
### Included in Scope (v1 MVP)
- Compute 'last qualifying engagement date' per Country as a derived query over existing Engagement records 
linked to any active (non-deactivated, non-merged) Contact of that Country. Engagement types that count come 
from one global config list that excludes automated mass notices and internal notes. No new table and no new 
fields for coordinators.
- Single cold rule: a Country is 'cold' when the later of its last qualifying Engagement and its Country 
creation/onboarding date is more than 90 days old. A newly onboarded country therefore cannot be cold until 90
days after onboarding. The threshold is one global config constant, not per-country.
- Suppress the cold flag only while the Country has a Mission with status Active whose record was created or 
last updated within the past 180 days (existing system timestamps). After that the country is flagged and the 
digest notes the stale active Mission. The 180-day cap is one global constant.
- Weekly digest sent to the Regional Coordinator assigned to the Country. It lists countries that newly turned
cold that week and those still cold. Country-level detail appears only in the in-app notification, behind 
login. The email contains only a count and a link, with no country names. Each digest is built per recipient 
by running the same Country-visibility query the Country page uses, evaluated as that recipient at send time, 
never with service-level privileges.
- Fallback routing: if the assigned coordinator is blank or deactivated, the cold country goes into an 
'unassigned' section of the digest for the existing Operations role group. Send failures are written to the 
existing job/error log. No new screen or per-country setting is added.
- The alert clears automatically when a new qualifying Engagement is logged. There is no acknowledge, snooze 
or approval step.
- Show a read-only 'Last engagement: <date> (cold)' indicator on the existing Country page. It is visible only
to users who already have access to that Country.
- Post-launch false-alert measurement as a derived query over existing data, with no new fields: the share of 
cold flags followed within 14 days by an Engagement dated before the flag date (backdated logging). It is 
reported weekly to the product owner.

### Pending Human Decision (relies on an escalated issue)
- None.

### Explicitly Excluded from Scope
- Sentiment analysis, relationship health scoring or any ML model
- Per-country, per-region or per-contact threshold settings
- New dashboards, queues or monitoring screens for cold countries or undelivered alerts
- Alert acknowledgement, 'acknowledged-but-not-logged' flags, snooze, escalation or approval workflows
- Per-country fallback recipients or delegate/leave-cover settings
- Per-country or per-mission suppression override flags
- Ingesting email, calendar or other external signals to infer engagement
- Alerts for individual contacts going stale or for ministry-level coldness
- New RBAC or permission model beyond the existing Country/Mission access
- Defining what counts as 'cold' diplomatically, which Engagement types reflect a real relationship, and 
whether country names may appear in email (policy and data-classification decisions for humans; v1 uses 
neutral, conservative, adjustable defaults)
- Backfilling or cleaning historical Engagement, Contact or Mission data

### Success Criteria
- In acceptance testing on production-like data, 100% of countries whose last qualifying Engagement (or 
onboarding date) is over 90 days old and that have no Active Mission updated within 180 days appear in the 
next weekly digest, and 0% of countries with a recent qualifying Engagement or a recently maintained active 
Mission do.
- Zero new required or manually maintained fields are added for Regional Coordinators. Alerting works entirely
from existing Engagement, Mission, Contact and Country-assignment data.
- Backtest on the last 12 months of Engagement data: on average no more than 20% of countries are flagged cold
in any given week. Above that, the threshold or rule is revisited before launch. Before the first send, 
Operations reviews the flagged list for about 10 countries with their coordinators as a one-time sanity check 
(no software), and the result is recorded.
- A cold indicator clears within 24 hours of a new qualifying Engagement being logged for that Country, with 
no user action.
- 100% of cold countries whose coordinator assignment is blank or points to a deactivated user appear in the 
Operations fallback digest in the next weekly run, verified in acceptance testing. Zero cold countries are 
dropped silently.
- Zero digests, emails or indicators expose a Country to a user who lacks existing access to it, verified by 
permission tests across at least 3 role types plus (a) the batch digest path, (b) a coordinator reassigned 
between two digest runs, and (c) an assertion that email bodies contain no country names.
- Within 4 weeks of launch, the weekly backdated-engagement query is reporting the false-alert rate. If more 
than 40% of cold flags are followed within 14 days by a backdated Engagement, an acknowledge/feedback 
mechanism is considered for the next release.

## 2. Surfaced Assumptions & Deliberation Outcomes
- **[A1] 'Cold' means elapsed time since the last logged qualifying Engagement with an active Contact of the 
Country (clock starting no earlier than Country onboarding). It is not a qualitative judgment of relationship 
quality.** (Challenged: `True` -> Disposition: `REVISED_AND_ACCEPTED`)
  - *Notes:* C5 was revised and accepted: only active, non-merged Contacts and a global list of qualifying 
Engagement types count, and the cold clock starts no earlier than onboarding.
- **[A2] The alert recipient is the Regional Coordinator already associated with the Country in the existing 
model. Operations and Country Engagement see the same data on the Country page.** (Challenged: `True` -> 
Disposition: `REVISED_AND_ACCEPTED`)
  - *Notes:* C1 was revised and accepted: cold countries with a blank or deactivated coordinator go to an 
Operations fallback section, and send failures are logged in the existing job log.
- **[A3] Engagement history in the CRM is reasonably complete and current. v1 adds no new data-capture burden 
and accepts some false 'cold' alerts where engagement happened but was not logged.** (Challenged: `True` -> 
Disposition: `REVISED_AND_ACCEPTED`)
  - *Notes:* C2 was revised and accepted: v1 still accepts some false cold alerts, but adds a one-time 
pre-launch spot check and a weekly backdated-engagement false-alert query with a 40% trigger for reconsidering
an acknowledge mechanism.
- **[A4] A recently maintained active Mission (Active status, created or updated within 180 days) in a Country
is sufficient evidence of an ongoing relationship, so that Country is not flagged cold even without a recent 
logged Engagement.** (Challenged: `True` -> Disposition: `REVISED_AND_ACCEPTED`)
  - *Notes:* C4 was revised and accepted: Mission suppression applies only to Active Missions created or 
updated within the past 180 days, so a forgotten Mission cannot silence a country indefinitely.
- **[A5] Existing Country/Mission access controls determine who may see a Country's cold status. Alerts add no
new visibility.** (Challenged: `True` -> Disposition: `REVISED_AND_ACCEPTED`)
  - *Notes:* C3 was revised and accepted: digests are built per recipient with the Country-page visibility 
query at send time, and emails carry only a count and a link with no country names.
- **[A6] A 90-day threshold is a reasonable product default for v1 and is not diplomatic policy. Stakeholders 
may change the single constant later.** (Challenged: `False` -> Disposition: `ACCEPTED`)
  - *Notes:* Not challenged; the 90-day threshold stays as one adjustable global constant and is accepted as a
product default, not diplomatic policy.

## 3. Open Questions Requiring Human / Policy Sign-Off
- None. No issue was escalated to humans.

